summary:
- topic: District Progress Report
  text: Superintendent Matheney highlighted significant district improvements, including
    decreased chronic absenteeism, a dramatic reduction in high school suspensions
    from 150 to 36 in one year, and improved SAT scores that now exceed the national
    average. He also noted strong academic growth in elementary schools, with three-quarters
    of Kaler students meeting or exceeding growth goals in math.
- topic: Retiree and Staff Recognition
  text: The board honored nine long-serving staff members retiring with a collective
    242 years of service. The board also recognized Monica Malcomson, a teacher at
    Brown School, as the 2025 Cumberland County Teacher of the Year for her outstanding
    contributions and creation of inclusive programs.
- topic: Community Partnerships Update
  text: The board heard a comprehensive report on the district's extensive community
    partnerships, which provide hundreds of volunteers, food security support through
    The Locker Project, and numerous career exploration opportunities (ELOs) for students.
    The administration also announced plans to establish a formal Education Foundation
    to further support school initiatives.
- topic: School Security Upgrades
  text: The Director of Operations reported that security camera systems will be installed
    in all five elementary schools over the summer. Additionally, bids for secure
    vestibules came in under budget, with construction potentially starting this summer
    at Brown, Kaler, and Small schools.
- topic: Federal Funding (ESEA) Update
  text: The Assistant Superintendent reported an unanticipated decrease in federal
    Title I and Title IV funding for the upcoming year, contributing to a cumulative
    loss of nearly $200,000 over three years. While the district absorbed the impact
    for the FY26 budget, the reduction means fewer dollars for professional development
    and raises concerns for future budgets.
- topic: Pre-K Partnership Update
  text: The district is moving forward with a partnership with Chickadee to fund 12
    pre-K seats for South Portland residents starting this fall. A planned partnership
    with United Way for additional seats has been delayed until fall 2026.
- topic: Public Comment and Board Communications
  text: Public comment included heartfelt tributes to retiring principal Diane Lang
    and music teacher Craig Uffington. Board members reiterated their commitment to
    student mental health, celebrated the recent high school graduation, and encouraged
    the community to vote in the upcoming school budget referendum.
